      <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Are Kindle lock-screen ads gone after I remove them?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Yes. The $20 ad-removal payment is permanent for that device. Once you pay it, Amazon stops serving any advertisements to that specific Kindle, including the lock-screen, sleep-screen, and home-screen "Recommended for you" strip. The ad-removal is tied to the device serial number, so a factory reset does not bring the ads back.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Can I tell whether a Kindle is "with ads" or "without ads" before I buy it?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Yes. On Amazon's product pages, the two SKUs are listed as separate purchase options under the same product, and the listing explicitly says either "With Special Offers" or "Without Special Offers" in the title. The price is shown next to each option. If you are shopping in a physical store like Best Buy, the box will have a sticker indicating the SKU. Refurbished Kindles sold by Amazon are typically the ad-free version unless explicitly stated otherwise.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Do the lock-screen ads change when I sideload EPUB files?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>No. The lock-screen ads are served by Amazon's advertising system and are independent of what is in your library. Sideloaded EPUB files converted with Calibre appear in your library identically whether you have the ad-supported or ad-free Kindle, and the reading experience inside each book is the same. Only the lock-screen, sleep-screen, and home-screen promotional strip differ between the two SKUs.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Why did Amazon raise Kindle prices in August 2026?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>The August 22, 2026 price hike on every Kindle except the Scribe was part of a broader Amazon device-price increase tied to the global memory and storage shortage. AI demand has absorbed most of the world's high-bandwidth memory and NAND flash production, driving up component costs for non-AI electronics. We covered the underlying memory story in <a href="/blog/amazon-kindle-price-hike-2026/">Amazon Raises Kindle Prices: Paperwhite Hits $199 in 2026</a> and the broader Amazon device range in <a href="/blog/amazon-memory-crisis-60-percent-device-price-hike-2026/">Amazon's Memory Crisis: 60% Device Price Hike 2026</a>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Is there a way to get a Kindle without ads for less than the $20 removal fee?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Occasionally. Amazon runs promotions during Prime Day, Black Friday, and back-to-school sales that drop the ad-removal fee from $20 to $0 or $10 for new buyers. The promotion has to be active at the moment you are paying for the ad-supported Kindle, and you only see the discount when you go to the ad-removal page. Buying a refurbished Kindle directly from Amazon is the most reliable way to get ad-free hardware at a lower price, since Amazon refurbished units are typically the ad-free SKU by default.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

